    "result_text": "New owners now get a `customerCode` when they're created, such as `SMI-0011`. The test suite passes (`./mvnw test`, 1 test, 0 failures). There's only one test: it checks the prefix and that two owners created in a row get numbers one apart. It runs on HSQLDB with the Spring Data JPA repository, so the JDBC and plain JPA versions of the count were never run.\n\n**What changed**\n- **Database:** added a `customer_code` column to `owners` in all four schema files (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres). Owners already in the seed data have no code.\n- **`Owner`:** new `customerCode` field with getter and setter, also added to `toString`.\n- **`OwnerRepository`:** new `long count()`, implemented for Spring Data JPA (with an explicit `@Query`), plain JPA, and JDBC. The JDBC repository's selects and update now read and write `customer_code` too.\n- **`ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner`:** when the owner is new, it sets the code as `<LAST3>-<count + 1, padded to 4 digits>`. Updating an owner keeps the existing code, even if the last name changes.\n- **API:** added `customerCode` to the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml` as read-only, so it's returned but ignored on input. The mapper skips it when building an owner from request fields.\n\n**Choices you may want to revisit**\n- **Letters only:** \"first three letters\" skips non-letters, so `O'Brien` becomes `OBR-\u2026`. A last name shorter than three letters gives a shorter prefix (`LI-0012`).\n- **Numbers can repeat:** because the number is just the current count plus one, two owners can get the same number. That happens if an owner is deleted (the count drops) or two owners are created at the same moment. I didn't put a unique constraint on the column for that reason. If codes must never repeat, a real database sequence would be the fix.\n- **Past 9999:** the number simply gets a fifth digit.",
